Remove the attack-mobility tier system and its directional-steering behavior.

Do not merely:

* Set every tier’s control to zero
* Set topSpeed to zero
* Force every attack to use planted
* Leave dead tier definitions behind
* Retain unused attack-mobility imports
* Hide the feature while leaving it active through another movement path
* Preserve the old system for CPUs
* Replace it with another differently named attack-steering system

Clean the attack schema, character data, runtime, CPU capability model, tests, and documentation.

If js/data/attack-mobility.js no longer serves a legitimate purpose, delete it and all references.

Do not remove ordinary locomotion or air control when the fighter is not performing a move. Only the attack-specific mobility system is being removed.

5.3 Preserve momentum without allowing steering

Removing Attack Steering does not mean every attack must freeze the fighter or erase all existing velocity.

These concepts are separate:

1. Preexisting momentum: Speed carried into an attack from running, jumping, falling, or a Dash.
2. Step-in: A one-time automatic forward impulse caused by the attack.
3. Scripted attack motion: A dash, roll, rise, plunge, rebound, etc., directly belonging to the move.
4. External forces: Knockback, pull, launch, stage movement, and related physics.
5. Attack Steering: New or redirected movement caused by held directional input during the attack.

Remove only category 5 as a player-controlled system.

The other categories must continue to behave according to their actual mechanics.

A fighter may still travel during an attack because:

* It entered the attack while already moving.
* It received an automatic Step-in.
* The move has scripted movement.
* Gravity is acting on it.
* An outside force moved it.
* A platform carries it.

None of those by itself constitutes Attack Steering.

An appropriate general solution is neutral, input-independent velocity evolution while the attack is active, using Alva’s existing physics.

Preserve truly authored entry-momentum behavior where mechanically essential.

For example:

* A move that deliberately retains incoming speed must still retain it.
* A move that deliberately starts stationary must still do so.
* A roll that scales from incoming run speed must still use that initial speed.
* A rebound must still apply its prescribed velocity.
* A lunge must still produce its prescribed impulse.

If old attack-mobility fields also store such genuinely authored non-steering mechanics, migrate only the necessary mechanics into a clearly separate, minimal representation.

Do not continue to depend on the old tier system to implement them.

5.4 Directional input must not control movement during an attack

Once an attack begins, left/right movement input must not:

* Accelerate the fighter
* Reverse its velocity
* Steer an automatic Step-in
* Add run speed to an attack
* Maintain speed using attack-specific held-direction bonuses
* Cancel passive deceleration merely because Run is held
* Redirect an aerial attack’s horizontal drift through the old mobility system
* Redirect a plunge or rise through attack steering
* Bypass movement commitment because lockMovement is false

The input may still affect initial attack facing when the move begins, according to Alva’s existing rules.

Preserve legitimate attack-facing behavior, Automatic turn-around, and CPU target-facing rules unless a specific change is needed to prevent movement redirection.

Facing and aiming are not automatically the same thing as horizontal Attack Steering.

Do not unintentionally redirect an already committed scripted movement or one-time Step-in because its sprite faces a different direction later.

5.5 Explicitly eliminate the lockMovement: false loophole

Currently, Fighter.update() can route attacks with lockMovement: false through normal locomotion instead of steerAttack().

That must no longer provide an attack-steering bypass.

Review whether lockMovement is still needed for anything other than the obsolete movement system.

If it is no longer necessary, remove or replace it cleanly.

If another non-steering mechanic still needs movement-state metadata, use clearly defined semantics that do not grant regular player locomotion during attack execution.

A move cannot remain steerable simply because it previously used lockMovement: false.

5.6 Remove steering from scripted-motion support paths

Inspect the implementation of:

homing
bounce
slam
rise
roll
hover

These attacks currently interact with moveAttack() or attack-mobility settings in different phases.

Preserve their automatic movement mechanisms.

However, where horizontal player steering currently comes from:

control
airControl
topSpeed
airTopSpeed

remove that directional influence.

Examples:

* A homing attack still homes automatically.
* A roll still moves by its scripted roll velocity.
* A rise still rises.
* A slam still falls and lands.
* A bounce still bounces.
* A hover still hovers.
* A grounded melee Step-in still moves forward once.

But holding Run during any of these attacks must not produce general Attack Steering.

A move’s scripted travel is not permission to retain the generic steering system.

5.7 Clean up steerAttack() appropriately

The existing steerAttack() combines:

1. One-time Step-in
2. Attack-specific directional movement

These must be separated.

Prefer a clean shared movement function or functions that implement:

* Resolved Step-in
* Existing passive momentum behavior
* Neutral physical deceleration
* Attack-specific scripted movement where applicable

But no attack-specific response to ongoing held movement direction.

Keep steer() available for ordinary, out-of-attack fighter locomotion.

Do not inadvertently remove universal walking, running, jumping, air control, turning, or Dash mechanics.

